// THE MAINSTREAM CALL POOL, HAND-CURATED — edit this file directly; NOTHING GENERATES IT.
//
// IT HOLDS THE WHOLE VOCABULARY, WHICH IS A STARTING POINT AND NOT AN ORDER. Every unique `<tam title>` the
// Mainstream corpus carries — 322 of them — in the corpus's own file order, so related calls sit together
// and the work is REARRANGING rather than typing. Trim it to the calls the ladder should teach, put them in the
// order it should teach them, and move `POOL_VERSION` with the result.
//
// It was generated by `game/tools/build-pool.mjs` until the owner took the order over by hand, and that script is
// deleted: the order the game teaches in is a curatorial act (DESIGN.md §5, §15 Q11), and both mechanical orders
// that were tried — the asset directory's own basename sort, and the CALLERLAB (New) Mainstream Suggested Teaching
// Order — are recorded in §15 Q11 with the numbers that rejected them. There is no generator to re-run, so an edit
// here CANNOT be overwritten.
//
// WHAT AN EDIT NEEDS FROM `game/test/pool-probe.mjs`, which runs in the gate:
//
//   1. every title names a call the corpus carries. A tam TITLE carries no formation, so a title several tams
//      share — `Circle Left 3/4` is on four, from four different starting formations — is ONE entry here and
//      stands for all of them;
//   2. no title appears twice: `poolFor` is the first `poolSize` entries, so a repeat takes two positions and
//      gives one call, shifting every later level's pool by one with no other symptom;
//   3. every title is one the LEVEL LOADER can register. MEASURED, and it is the number to look at BEFORE this
//      list is played rather than after: the loader (`src/data/corpus-node.ts`) names each FILE after its FIRST
//      tam, so it can draw 62 of these 322 titles, and each of the other 260 registers nothing and
//      SILENTLY shrinks the level that asks for it.
//
// A TITLE HERE IS AN IDENTITY, NOT A POSITION: registration in the game is by title, so RE-ORDERING THIS ARRAY
// renumbers every level's pool — `poolFor` is the first `poolSize` entries — and MUST come with a `POOL_VERSION`
// bump, because the version is part of what a leaderboard entry means (DESIGN.md §7).
